  "explain": {
   "text": "A fraction like 3/4 is a single number, not a 3 and a 4.[^1]\n\nThe bottom number tells you the size of the pieces. A 4 means the whole is cut into four equal pieces, called quarters.[^2]\n\nThe top number tells you how many of those pieces to count. Count three quarters, and where you stop is 3/4.[^3]\n\nTry it below. Each quarter you add shades in more of the bar and moves the mark along the line.\n\nKeep going past four quarters and you pass 1. Seven quarters is one whole and three quarters more, which we write as 1 3/4.\n\n**The bottom number names the piece. The top number counts the pieces.**"
  },
  "analogy": null,
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** If the top is smaller than the bottom, the fraction is less than 1. If they match, it is exactly 1. If the top is bigger, it is more than 1.\n\n**Worked example:** A drawing puts a hole at 1 3/8 inches. Go to the 1 inch mark, then count on three eighths.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Thinking bigger numbers mean a bigger fraction. 5/8 is less than 3/4.\n- Thinking a fraction can't be more than 1."
  },
